        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Tyranny</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 657 BCE – c. 146 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Power held by</span><span class="info-stat-val">A single ruler, the tyrannos, backed by a bodyguard or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, with relatives and allies in the main offices and the city’s laws and magistracies often left in place</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Legitimacy from</span><span class="info-stat-val">None by law or ancestral right: power won by force, fraud or popular support against the ruling aristocrats, and kept by results, patronage, religious display and fear</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How rulers are chosen</span><span class="info-stat-val">Seized by coup, often with popular or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> backing; usually passed to sons but seldom beyond them; ended by assassination, revolt or the intervention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and other powers</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Famous examples</span><span class="info-stat-val">Corinth under the Cypselids, Sicyon under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthagorids">Orthagorids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> under the Peisistratids (546–510 BCE), Samos under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>, Syracuse under Dionysius I (405–367 BCE)</span></div>
        </div>
