    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Greek tyrants were men who seized sole power in a city-state outside its traditional order, often by championing the common people against a narrow aristocracy.</li><li>Some, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a> of Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, presided over prosperity, public building and new festivals, but few tyrannies outlasted the founder’s sons.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> made the tyrant the type of the worst ruler, governing for himself and without law, and their judgement fixed the meaning the word has had ever since.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> after his first exile by entering the city in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> beside a tall woman named Phye, dressed in armour as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, while heralds proclaimed that the goddess was bringing him home.</p></aside>
    </section>

