    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 657 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a> overthrows the Bacchiad aristocracy and makes himself tyrant of Corinth (traditional date)</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">546 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> defeats the Athenians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallene">Pallene</a> and begins his third and longest tyranny</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">514 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Harmodius and Aristogeiton kill Hipparchus; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> drives out his brother Hippias in 510 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon of Syracuse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theron-of-acragas">Theron of Acragas</a> defeat a Carthaginian army at Himera</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">405 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Dionysius I seizes power in Syracuse during a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and rules until 367 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">404 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> installs the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a>, an oligarchy that rules <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> by terror until 403 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">192 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis</a>, the last independent ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, is assassinated and Sparta is forced into the Achaean League</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">146 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroys Corinth and brings the cities of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> under Roman control</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peisistratus.jpg" alt="Peisistratus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Peisistratos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Statens Museum for Kunst via Europeana website, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A tyrant, in the Greek sense, was a man who made himself sole ruler of a city-state by means outside its customary order. The word described how power had been won, not how it was used: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called both usurpers and the sons who inherited their rule tyrants, and in the Archaic period the title carried little of its later reproach. Most tyrants came from the aristocracy. They rose by exploiting feuds among the leading families and the grievances of poorer citizens, small farmers and the newly prosperous, and many had military reputations: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> had captured the Megarian port of Nisaea before he made his bid for power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><p>Once in power a tyrant ruled through force, patronage and continuity. He kept a bodyguard, often of foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>; Peisistratus began with a guard of club-bearers voted him by the Athenian assembly and later relied on hired troops paid from the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of the Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pangaion">Pangaeus</a> region. He sent rivals into exile, held their children as hostages, placed relatives and friends in the city’s offices and made alliances by marriage and with tyrants in other cities. Yet many tyrants left the laws and magistracies in place. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Peisistratus governed without disturbing the existing offices or changing the laws, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> wrote that his family took care only that one of their own always held office. Tyrants taxed harvests, built temples, fountains and harbours, founded festivals, struck coins and invited poets to their courts.</p><p>Greek political thinkers set tyranny against kingship. A king, in their view, ruled willing subjects according to law and ancestral custom and for the common good; a tyrant ruled unwilling subjects for his own advantage and above the law. Tyranny also differed from the office of the aisymnetes, a ruler given extraordinary powers by the citizens’ own choice, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus of Mytilene</a>, whose office <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called “an elective tyranny”. The so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a> who ruled Athens in 404–403 BCE were in fact an oligarchy, a board of thirty men given the name for their lawless violence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/archilochus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archilochus.jpg" alt="Archilochus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/archilochus">Archilochus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archilochus_01_pushkin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shakko, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tyranny appeared in the Greek world in the 7th century BCE, as the city-states grew richer and their ruling aristocracies narrower. Overseas trade and the founding of colonies brought new wealth, and changes in warfare gave more weight to the citizens who could afford a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a>’s armour; historians such as Antony Andrewes have seen in these men the backing on which the first tyrants relied, though the theory is debated. Feuds within the aristocracy gave ambitious men their chance. At Corinth the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacchiadae">Bacchiadae</a>, a single clan that married only among themselves and governed the city, were overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a>, traditionally in 657 BCE, though some historians prefer a date several decades later; he killed or exiled members of the clan and ruled, it was said, without a bodyguard. Orthagoras founded a dynasty at Sicyon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theagenes-of-megara">Theagenes of Megara</a> seized power in his city, and in 636 or 632 BCE the Olympic victor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cylon-of-athens">Cylon of Athens</a>, Theagenes’ son-in-law, failed in an attempt to seize the Acropolis.</p><p>The word tyrannos was probably borrowed: it may have come from Lydian or another language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and one of its earliest uses is by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archilochus">Archilochus</a>, writing about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia">Gyges of Lydia</a>, who had killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candaules">Candaules</a> and taken his throne and his queen. In the Archaic period the word meant simply a monarch who had come to power without hereditary or constitutional right, and poets could use it of kings: the hero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a> Tyrannus is called tyrannos because he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> from outside and won its throne, not because he was cruel.</p><p>The first writers to describe tyranny were the historians and philosophers of the 5th and 4th centuries BCE, who wrote after democracy had made it hateful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> told the stories of Cypselus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> in his Histories, and gave the Corinthian Socles a speech calling tyranny the most unjust and bloodthirsty thing among men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>’s Republic traced tyranny’s birth from the excesses of democracy, observing that the people always set up some champion whom they nurse into greatness, and that this is the root from which a tyrant springs: when he first appears, he is a protector. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>’s Hiero imagined the tyrant of Syracuse confessing his unhappiness to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s Politics analysed how tyrannies arose, how they were preserved and why they fell.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polycrates"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polycrates.jpg" alt="Polycrates" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polykrates_with_Pharao_Amasis_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author Published in 1890, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The age of the tyrants reached its height in the 6th century BCE. At Corinth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a> was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander</a>, under whom Corinth’s trade and colonies flourished, but Periander’s successor was soon expelled and an oligarchy took power. At Sicyon the Orthagorid tyranny was at its most powerful under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes-of-sicyon">Cleisthenes of Sicyon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates of Samos</a> built a great fleet, until the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oroetes">Oroetes</a> lured him to the mainland and killed him around 522 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> seized power about 561 BCE, was twice driven out and twice returned, the second time in 546 BCE, when he landed at Marathon with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and routed the Athenians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallene">Pallene</a>. His sons Hippias and Hipparchus succeeded him in 528 or 527 BCE. After Harmodius and Aristogeiton killed Hipparchus in 514 BCE, Hippias grew harsher, and in 510 BCE a Spartan army drove him out; the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> followed in 508/507 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> won much of its leadership in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> by deposing tyrants, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> backed tyrants in the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> until those cities expelled them at the start of their revolt in 499 BCE.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> the threat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> kept tyranny alive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon</a>, master of Gela, took Syracuse in 485 BCE and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theron-of-acragas">Theron of Acragas</a> defeated a Carthaginian army at Himera in 480 BCE; his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-i-of-syracuse">Hiero I of Syracuse</a> welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacchylides">Bacchylides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a> to his court. Syracuse became a democracy in 466 BCE, but in 405 BCE, during a new war with Carthage, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I of Syracuse</a> persuaded the assembly to grant him a bodyguard and ruled until his death in 367 BCE. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-ii-of-syracuse">Dionysius II of Syracuse</a> was driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> in 344 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles of Syracuse</a>, who seized power in 317 BCE, took the title of king in 304 BCE.</p><p>In Athens the name returned in 404 BCE, when Sparta installed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a>, whom democrats returning from exile overthrew the next year. Under the Macedonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a>, tyrants backed by Macedonia ruled Peloponnesian cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argos">Argos</a>, Megalopolis and Sicyon. From 251 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus of Sicyon</a> freed his city and joined it to the Achaean League, the league drove them out or persuaded them to step down: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydiadas">Lydiadas</a> of Megalopolis laid down his tyranny in 235 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis</a>, the last independent ruler of Sparta, was assassinated in 192 BCE and Sparta was forced into the league; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroyed Corinth in 146 BCE, southern Greece came under Roman control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos.jpg" alt="Tunnel of Eupalinos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos">Tunnel of Eupalinos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eupalinian_aqueduct.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Moumouza at English Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancient writers who had little love for tyranny still credited some tyrants with good government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> said that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> at first ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> fairly and well. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> wrote that the Peisistratids cultivated wisdom and virtue, taxed the Athenians only a twentieth of their produce, beautified the city, carried on its wars and kept up its sacrifices. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s Constitution of the Athenians described Peisistratus as mild and forgiving, governing more like a citizen than a tyrant: he lent money to poor farmers so that they could work their land, sent judges round the countryside and once appeared in court himself to answer a charge of murder, and men later called his rule “the age of Cronus”, a golden age.</p><p>The record supports some of this praise. Under their tyrants Corinth, Samos and Athens prospered, and their public works served the whole city. Peisistratus and his sons built fountain houses that gave the people public water where aristocrats had kept private <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, began the vast Temple of Olympian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaea</a> and founded or enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysia">City Dionysia</a>, where the first tragedies were staged around 534 BCE; tradition also credits them with the first attempt at a fixed text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>, Samos gained a harbour mole, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos">Tunnel of Eupalinos</a>, more than a kilometre long, which carried water through a mountain to the city, and a vast new temple at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion of Samos</a>, the three works that Herodotus counted the greatest in the Greek world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon</a> and Dionysius I defended Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><p>Modern historians have stressed the tyrants’ place in the political development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. By breaking the grip of the old aristocratic clans, centring cults and festivals on the city and easing the grievances of poorer citizens, the tyrants helped to turn loose groupings of villages and families into unified cities. Sarah Pomeroy and her co-authors describe the Peisistratid tyranny as a levelling force for all who stood outside the tyrant’s circle, which eased the way for the democracy that followed in Athens, and Antony Andrewes saw the tyrants as a stage in the passing of power from narrow aristocracies to the wider body of citizens.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plato"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="Plato" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The philosophers built their theory of tyranny as the worst of governments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>’s Republic portrayed the tyrant as the most wretched of men, enslaved to his own appetites, friendless and afraid, and the tyrannical city as the most enslaved of cities, since in both reason had lost control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s Politics ranked tyranny as the worst perversion of rule by one man and catalogued the methods by which tyrants kept power: cutting down outstanding men, forbidding common meals and clubs, keeping spies, sowing distrust among citizens, waging war so that the people would always need a leader, and impoverishing their subjects so that they had no leisure to conspire. He counted the great building projects of tyrants among these means, citing the pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the Peisistratids’ Temple of Olympian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a> on Samos. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> told how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrasybulus-of-miletus">Thrasybulus of Miletus</a>, asked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander</a> for advice, said nothing but cut off the tallest ears in a field of corn.</p><p>The record of cruelty was long. Periander was said to have killed his wife Melissa and to have sent three hundred boys of Corcyra to be castrated; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalaris">Phalaris</a> of Acragas was remembered for roasting his victims alive in a bronze bull; Hippias, after his brother’s murder, put many Athenians to death; and the tale of the Sword of Damocles, set at the court of Dionysius of Syracuse, made the tyrant’s constant fear proverbial. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a>, though an oligarchy, executed some 1,500 Athenians without trial, killed men for their property and forced Theramenes, one of their own number, to drink hemlock.</p><p>Tyranny was also unstable. Resting on no recognised right, the tyrant depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, on foreign allies and on his own success, and the sons who inherited his power rarely kept it. Aristotle observed that most tyrannies were short-lived and that the longest, that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthagorids">Orthagorids</a> at Sicyon, lasted about a hundred years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a>, asked what was the strangest thing he had seen, is said to have answered “an aged tyrant”. Exiled tyrants turned to their cities’ enemies: Hippias went to the Persian court and guided the Persian army to Marathon in 490 BCE, and the tyrants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>’s agents. Later tyrants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> held power as clients of the Macedonian kings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="Cicero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Cicero_(1st-cent._BC)_-_Palazzo_Nuovo_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Greek experience gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> its word and its theory of illegitimate power. Athenian democracy defined itself against tyranny. It honoured Harmodius and Aristogeiton with statues in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">Agora</a> as the city’s liberators, introduced ostracism, first used in 487 BCE, which could send a citizen who seemed too powerful into exile for ten years, and in 336 BCE passed a law, inscribed on a stone that survives, protecting anyone who killed a man trying to set up a tyranny. Roman writers set tyranny against liberty: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, in On Duties, praised the killing of tyrants and counted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> among them.</p><p>Medieval and early modern thinkers sharpened the distinction the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> had drawn. John of Salisbury’s Policraticus (1159) defended the killing of tyrants, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> held that one who killed a usurping tyrant to free his country, where there was no higher authority to appeal to, deserved praise and reward. The jurist Bartolus of Sassoferrato, in his treatise On Tyranny of the 1350s, separated the two meanings the word had acquired: a tyrant by defect of title, who seized power he had no right to, and a tyrant by conduct, a lawful ruler who governed unjustly. Later political thought has mostly used the second sense. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>’s Two Treatises of Government defined tyranny as “the exercise of power beyond right” and declared that “wherever law ends, tyranny begins”, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence</a> charged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> with seeking “an absolute Tyranny”. In On Tyranny (1948), a commentary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>’s Hiero, the philosopher Leo Strauss argued that modern political science had failed to recognise the tyrannies of its own century, and that the classical analysis of tyranny was needed to understand them.</p><p>Modern historians have tried to see the archaic tyrants apart from the later legend. Some, such as Greg Anderson, argue that many early “tyrants” were powerful aristocrats whose rule was recast as usurpation only later, in the ideology of democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and James McGlew has studied how the image of the tyrant shaped Greek ideas of citizenship and freedom. Most now see the tyrants neither as the monsters of philosophical theory nor simply as champions of the people, but as ambitious aristocrats whose rule could leave cities such as Corinth, Athens and Syracuse stronger and richer than they found them.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Greek tyranny was the rule of one man who seized power in a city-state outside its traditional order. Between the 7th and 2nd centuries BCE tyrants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> and Dionysius I brought prosperity, building and war but rarely founded lasting dynasties, and their enemies, from Athenian democrats to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, made the tyrant the enduring image of lawless power.</em></p>

